Touraine food specialities come in three families: pork, goat's cheese and white wine. Rillettes, rillons, Sainte-Maure, Vouvray, with a nougat that is not a nougat to finish. I am Italian, I have cooked in Touraine since we arrived at the Château de la Huberdière, near Amboise, and these are the ten flavours I give everyone who asks me where to start.

## Which Touraine specialities should you taste first?

The ten Touraine specialities to taste are rillettes de Tours, rillons, beuchelle, géline, fouée, Sainte-Maure cheese, poire tapée, nougat de Tours, the macarons of Cormery and the wines of the valley.

Touraine does not have a showy cuisine, and that is what I like about it. It is a cuisine of produce, made to be eaten with bread and a glass of white. Tours was named an International City of Gastronomy in 2013, alongside Lyon, Dijon and Paris-Rungis, following UNESCO's listing of the French gastronomic meal.

Two of these products carry an official label from INAO, the French body for designations of origin: rillettes de Tours as a PGI and Sainte-Maure-de-Touraine as a PDO. Buy those first, because the label guarantees both the origin and the method.

## What is the traditional dish of Touraine?

The oldest traditional dish of Touraine is beuchelle, but day to day the region mostly eats pork: rillettes, rillons and andouillette cooked in Vouvray.

![Long table laid in the château's panelled dining room, French window open onto the grounds](/images/inline/restauration/restauration-02.webp "In the evening at La Huberdière, the boards bring together local cheeses and charcuterie. No beuchelle: we leave that to the traditional restaurants.")

### 1. PGI rillettes de Tours

Rillettes de Tours are slow-cooked in their own fat, then browned at the end. They keep long fibres and an amber colour that set them apart from rillettes du Mans, which are paler and finer. Spread them cold on country bread, never on soft white toast.

### 2. Rillons de Touraine

Rillons are large cubes of pork belly, confit and then browned. They are served warm, sliced, with a salad and warm goat's cheese: that is the salade tourangelle of every bistro. If you taste only one thing at lunch, make it this.

### 3. Beuchelle à la tourangelle

Veal sweetbreads and kidneys, mushrooms, cream: beuchelle is the region's celebration dish. It is getting rare on menus, so when you see it, order it.

### 4. Géline de Touraine

The géline is a black hen of a local breed, saved from extinction by dedicated breeders. Its meat is firm and full of flavour. A few poulterers sell it, and the restaurants that cook local serve it, often roasted or as a ballotine.

### 5. Fouée

A fouée is a small ball of bread dough baked in a wood-fired oven: it puffs up, you split it open and fill it with rillettes, butter or white beans. It is village-fête food more than restaurant food, and it is all the better for it.

## What are the local products of Touraine?

The two most emblematic local products of Touraine are Sainte-Maure-de-Touraine, a raw-milk goat's cheese, and poire tapée from Rivarennes, a pear dried in the oven and then flattened.

### 6. Sainte-Maure-de-Touraine PDO

Sainte-Maure is a log-shaped goat's cheese with a rye straw through the middle, engraved with the producer's name. Young, it is mild and almost lemony. Aged and ashed, it gains real strength. It is the first cheese I put on a board, and the one my Italian relatives ask for on every visit.

### 7. Poire tapée from Rivarennes

The pears are dried in the oven, then flattened with a mallet to press out the air. They keep for months. You rehydrate them in red Chinon, and the result is a winter dessert I am very fond of, halfway between a compote and candied fruit.

## What is the typical dessert of Tours?

The typical dessert of Tours is nougat de Tours, a shortcrust cake with apricot jam, candied fruit and an almond macaroon topping, nothing to do with the nougat of Montélimar.

### 8. Nougat de Tours

The name fools everyone the first time. It is a travel cake that keeps for several days, with a crunchy almond top and a soft apricot centre. You will find it in pastry shops in Tours and Amboise, whole or by the slice.

### 9. The macarons of Cormery

Nothing like Parisian macarons either. Those from Cormery are ring-shaped, with a light crust and a soft almond centre. They are made only in Cormery, half an hour south of Tours.

![Breakfast laid on the terrace in low sunlight, croissants in a basket, floral china and a silver jug](/images/inline/restauration/restauration-10.webp "Breakfast at the château is served from 8.30 to 10.30am, €15 per person, with homemade granola.")

## Which wines go with Touraine food?

With Touraine food you drink white first: Vouvray and Montlouis-sur-Loire, both Chenin Blanc, then a red Chinon or a Touraine-Amboise for game and aged cheeses.

### 10. Vouvray, Montlouis and Touraine-Amboise

The Chenin of Vouvray and Montlouis comes dry, medium-dry and sparkling. A dry Vouvray with rillettes, a medium-dry with an aged Sainte-Maure: those are the two pairings I recommend without hesitation. Touraine-Amboise is quieter, drunk locally at the village winemakers and rarely found elsewhere.

The vineyards begin almost at the end of our 14 hectares of grounds. The cellars, dug into the tufa stone, are a quarter of an hour from the château, and the cool of the rock hits you on the first step.

## What to eat in Amboise?

In Amboise, the specialities are those of all Touraine, with one advantage: the Sunday morning market, on the quay by the Loire, brings them all together in a few hundred metres.

Goat's cheeses, rillettes and rillons, Touraine strawberries in spring, Vouvray and Montlouis wines: this is where I do my shopping and where I send our guests on a Sunday. Get there before 10am, after that you will be elbowing your way through. The rest of the day is covered in our guide to [things to do in Amboise](/en/blog/things-to-do-amboise).

The local wine is called Touraine-Amboise, and it is the only product that really carries the town's name. The village cellars offer tastings, often without booking in summer.

## What to bring back from Tours or Amboise?

For a food souvenir from Tours or Amboise, choose a jar of PGI rillettes, a bottle of Vouvray, a nougat de Tours and a bag of poires tapées, all of which travel without a cool bag.

Sainte-Maure travels too, but chilled and for two days at most. A tip from experience: buy it on your last day, not your first. For rillettes, a charcutier who makes their own beats a souvenir-shop jar.

## And at the château's table, what comes from Touraine?

At La Huberdière's table, Touraine arrives through its produce: local cheeses and charcuterie on the evening boards, vegetables from the kitchen garden, and wines from the valley.

![Tray of green balls rolled by hand, lined up on baking paper before cooking](/images/inline/restauration/restauration-07.webp "Everything is made by hand, on site. The kitchen is not available to guests: it is where we work.")

I would rather be clear: we do not serve beuchelle and I do not make my own rillettes. My cooking is Italian, and on Thursday evenings the dinner is entirely so. What I have learnt here is to put a Sainte-Maure next to Parma ham and a Vouvray next to my pasta, and to find that they get along very well.

The table is served in the evening to guests staying at the château: every night except Sunday from 1 May to 30 September, and on request for six people or more the rest of the year. Everything is explained on the page about [dining at the château](/en/chateau-dining-touraine), and our article on the [table d'hôtes in the Loire](/en/blog/table-dhotes-loire-chateau) describes the Thursday Italian dinner.

If you want to taste Touraine by day and dine on site in the evening, [book your stay at the château](/en/boutique-hotel-amboise), 2 hrs 20 from Paris. For the more active, [cycling the Loire from Amboise](/en/blog/loire-valley-cycling-amboise) takes you past the cellars of Vouvray and Montlouis. Send us your dates: you will receive a proposal within 24 hours.
